|---|---:|
| hidden | 1.008680985915 |
| output | 1.000000027842 |
\image(figures/spectral_gan_norms.svg)
// result_end
\caption(The audit repeats the original Float64 arithmetic and denominator floor. The hidden point sits above the unit reference.)
\alttext(Measured final hidden and output effective-kernel SVD norms, with a horizontal unit reference.)

> Verification and limits

The complete native program matches all seven original archives/764429 values exactly
in dtype, shape and value bytes, both source PNGs, both TSVs and every non-timing metric.
Ten additional first-phase probe archives match364381 native values bit for bit across
initialization, warm-up, cache advance, initial/advanced gradients, D write and fresh G
write. The frozen source and helper snapshots remain beside those verification receipts.

An independent NumPy checker imports no datamunge and does no fitting. It checks339561
forward values,129554 parameter derivative values across the first phases,22 scalar finite
differences,16 warm-up iterations,849 state values,52418 exact checkpoint values and all3000
batch-row visits. It separately reproduces both final SVD audits and ownership controls.
Native, Python and R component checks cover owned records, invalid inputs, live denominator
paths and caller-owned updates. Those checks do not complete separate Python/R experiment loops.

This is one fixed short run without a seed sweep, uncertainty analysis or paper-scale image
benchmark. Perfect real/fake source separation here coexists with weak generated samples;
the repeated fitting mean still scores much better on the coarse diagnostic. Estimated
spectral normalization explains a computation and update schedule; these results establish
neither realistic generation, convergence nor a certified Lipschitz bound.
